          "text": "You’re right that **trusted publishing is supported** in `@semantic-release/npm` – but that support is about **how npm authenticates once a publish happens**, not about skipping the plugin’s own pre‑flight checks.\n\nTwo separate layers are in play:\n\n- **1. `@semantic-release/npm`’s `verifyConditions` step**  \n  The README you pasted still says:\n\n  > *verifyConditions – Verify the presence of the `NPM_TOKEN` environment variable, or an `.npmrc` file, and verify the authentication method is valid.*\n\n  Your stack trace is exactly from this code path (`set-npmrc-auth.js` → `verify-auth.js` → `ENONPMTOKEN`). That logic currently **refuses to proceed if neither `NPM_TOKEN` nor an auth’d `.npmrc` is present**, even if trusted publishing is configured.\n\n- **2. npm’s trusted publishing / OIDC**  \n  Those docs describe how, **after** auth is set up, npm will use OIDC instead of a long‑lived token and automatically add provenance. That works at the registry level, but it doesn’t remove the plugin’s requirement to see *some* auth configured first.\n\nBecause of that:\n\n- **Upgrading to `cycjimmy/semantic-release-action@v6` or pinning a newer `semantic_version` does not bypass `NPM_TOKEN`**; you’d still hit `ENONPMTOKEN` from `@semantic-release/npm`.\n- To be completely tokenless, you’d have to **stop using `@semantic-release/npm` for npmjs** and own `npm publish` yourself (what we tried earlier via `@semantic-release/exec`, which still hit `ENEEDAUTH`).\n\nGiven today’s plugin behavior, the only stable options are:\n\n- Provide a narrowly scoped **npm automation token** as `NPM_TOKEN`, or  \n- Drop `@semantic-release/npm` and keep debugging manual `npm publish` + OIDC.\n\nThe docs you quoted are about **what auth mechanism npm uses**, not about removing the plugin’s `NPM_TOKEN`/`.npmrc` guard.",
